## Why #4471 needed two tickets

The desk merged reports by subject, but matching subjects did not mean every report had the
same cause. The export contains overlapping rows, so the cause table counts each report ID
once and uses the tenant's locale, booking form and report channel. Intake tags are guesses.

## Cause 1: the shared parser flips day-first dates

The shared parser reads month first. For day-first tenants, dates can therefore land on the
wrong day. The export's one-day-move reports still belong to this cause under the tenant
rules. Track this cause in [issue #1](https://github.com/spamowaskrzynkamailowa-ship-it/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1).

## Cause 2: booking-v3 gives day-first tenants a conflicting hint

The v3 form displays `MM/DD/YYYY` for tenants whose locale writes day first. A customer can
follow that hint while the tenant-side parser reads the date in the opposite order. Track it
separately in [issue #2](https://github.com/spamowaskrzynkamailowa-ship-it/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).

## Counts from the export

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table de-duplicates by report ID and groups from tenant configuration and channel. The
five reports noted as one-day moves remain in the parser group; they do not establish a third
cause.

## Engineering outcome

The desk closed #4471 on 5 October. Engineering fixed the shared parser with
`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`. The v3-form issue will not be fixed because
`booking-v3` was retired.

